######
#
# Atsynmark: for marking ATS syntax
#
######

The command [pats2xhtml] is for syntax-hilighting level-1 ATS2 syntax
trees: it turns ATS2 source into XHTML (the keywords, comments, external
code, static and dynamic expressions, labels and strings are in <span>s).

The command line:

  pats2xhtml [--embedded] -s|--static <file> ...   # a .sats file
  pats2xhtml [--embedded] -d|--dynamic <file> ...  # a .dats file
  pats2xhtml [--embedded] -d|-s                    # the input is STDIN
  pats2xhtml -o|--output <file> ...                # the output goes to <file>
  pats2xhtml -h|--help

[--embedded] gives the <pre> element only (no HTML head). See the usage text.
The same is available as a library: utils/libatsynmark (for example
string_pats2xhtmlize_bground, which doc/EXAMPLE/EFFECTIVATS/MYTEXT calls).

######

Building (ATS1 is not needed):

  make                  # in this directory (or: make -C utils/atsynmark)

builds BUILD/pats2xhtml with patsopt and the C compiler (see Makefile_build and
../libatsynmark/Makefile_vars for the variables PATSHOME, PATSOPT, CC,
LIBATSOPT), links it with BUILD/libatsynmark.a (../libatsynmark, built first
if it is missing) and libatsopt.a (src/libatsopt.a, built first if it is
missing: the compiler as a library), and copies it to $(PATSHOME)/bin.

  make test             # the md5 suite of ../libatsynmark/TEST (needs python3)

Please note that the code in this directory is written in ATS2 without GC
(CLAUDE.md, "No GC"): the lexer, the parser and the syntax trees are those of
the compiler in src/ (written in ATS2 too; it used to be the ATS1 modules).
The output is the same as that of the ATS1 version (src/PORTING.md, section
"pats2xhtml and libatsynmark").

###### end of [README for Atsynmark] ######
